  (:db/id -1),
  (:adr/body "# ADR 2790003352: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 3352 Government Tax and Excise Officials documentation & logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatching hardware itself, always escalating\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 3352 --\nGovernment Tax and Excise Officials** only.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"3352\" ...}` entry was\nverified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M -e\n\"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-occupation\n\\\"3352\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/technology` sibling\npresent), and its `:name` read exactly \"Government Tax and Excise\nOfficials\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the\nentry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies\n[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` (no `:forms`) and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3352` (`gh api` 404-confirmed). This ADR\nand its companion registry edit promote the `3352` entry to\n`:implemented`. `wave-of \"3352\"` (`kotoba.occupation.wave`) resolves to\nwave 2, same as sibling ISCO 3313/3333/3343.\n\n**Government Tax and Excise Officials carry real legal authority to\nassess tax liability, impose penalties and order audits/collections.**\nThis ADR's task brief was explicit and doubled-down twice that this\nactor must NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or\npropose exercising ANY of that authority -- it is a documentation/\nlogistics-coordination robot ONLY, with no op anywhere in its allowlist\nresembling finalizing an assessment, imposing a penalty, or ordering a\ncollection/lien action. That constraint drove every domain decision\nbelow; it is treated as the single most important invariant of this\nrepository, ranked above structural mirroring of the reference.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for STRUCTURE (module shape,\ngraph topology, doc set) is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals), independently re-cloned and read in\nfull in this session, then adapted SUBSTANTIALLY for the domain per the\nenforcement-authority guidance above -- this is not a find/replace port,\nthe op vocabulary, entity model and hard-check set are new.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3352` as an OSS tax/excise-office\nfiling-documentation and logistics-coordination actor, following the SAME\ngoverned-actor architecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in\nthis fleet: **TaxFilingAdvisor ⊣ TaxExciseGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically\nto the reference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with an\nindependent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`,\nand an append-only audit ledger. All source is `.cljc` (no JVM-only\ninterop). No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to `deps.edn`\n(only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry itself depends on it,\nnot individual actor repos -- confirmed by re-reading the reference's own\n`deps.edn`, matching this batch's other sibling ADRs' independent finding\nof the same fact).\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`) -- structurally, not just gated\n\n- `:log-filing-record` -- filing/return-receipt data entry (documentation\n  only, never an assessment)\n- `:schedule-audit-appointment` -- audit/review appointment scheduling\n  proposal (a staffing/logistics ceiling check only -- NEVER determines\n  the audit's outcome)\n- `:flag-compliance-concern` -- surface an observation that MAY warrant\n  human tax-official review (discrepancy, late-filing pattern, risk\n  indicator); ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- office-equipment procurement proposal\n\nThere is no `:finalize-tax-assessment`, `:impose-penalty`,\n`:order-collection-action` or `:order-lien-action` op anywhere in this\ncodebase for the advisor to propose or the governor to gate -- adding one\nwould require editing this repository's source; no proposal content can\nreach it at runtime. This is the literal meaning of \"structurally absent,\nnot merely gated\" from the task brief.\n\n### Seven HARD governor checks (`taxexcise.governor/check`, permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **`:op-not-allowlisted`** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op\n   allowlist (`taxexcise.governor/allowed-ops`) is a HARD, PERMANENT\n   block. This is the primary structural mechanism realizing the\n   no-enforcement-authority guarantee: a hand-crafted proposal with\n   `:op :finalize-tax-assessment` is rejected purely because the governor\n   does not recognize the op, independent of any content inspection.\n2. **`:no-actuation`** -- any `:effect` other than `:propose` is a HARD\n   block.\n3. **`:no-filer`** -- filer-scoped ops (`:log-filing-record`,\n   `:flag-compliance-concern`) require an independently\n   verified/registered `taxexcise.store/filer` record, looked up from the\n   REQUEST (not trusted from the advisor's proposal echo).\n4. **`:no-office`** -- office-scoped ops (`:schedule-audit-appointment`,\n   `:coordinate-supply-order`) require an independently\n   verified/registered `taxexcise.store/office` record, same\n   request-sourced lookup discipline as (3). Together (3)+(4) realize the\n   task brief's \"official/office record must be independently verified/\n   registered before any action\" invariant.\n5. **`:missing-return-document`** -- `:log-filing-record` without\n   `:return-document-attached? true` is a HARD block (an unattested\n   filing log is a fabricated record, not routine documentation) --\n   directly modeled on the reference's source-document-attached check,\n   renamed to this domain's return/receipt-document vocabulary.\n6. **`:appointment-capacity-exceeded`** -- `:schedule-audit-appointment`\n   is HARD-blocked once the office's committed same-op record count\n   (`taxexcise.store/records-of-office`) reaches its registered\n   `:daily-appointment-capacity`. This is a deliberate domain adaptation\n   of the reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check: it is a pure\n   staffing/logistics ceiling on how many appointment SLOTS can be booked\n   -- it never evaluates, scores or determines what happens IN an audit,\n   only whether the office has capacity to schedule one more.\n7. **`:enforcement-scope-violation`** -- a SECOND, independent\n   defense-in-depth layer (`taxexcise.governor/enforcement-scope-phrases`,\n   scanned via `enforcement-scope-violation?` over every proposal's\n   `:rationale` and `:concern-detail` free-text fields, case-insensitive\n   substring match). Even though no enforcement OP can be proposed (check\n   1), a broken or adversarial advisor could still narrate performing one\n   inside an ALLOWED op's free text (e.g. a `:log-filing-record` proposal\n   whose rationale claims to \"finalize the tax assessment\"); this scan\n   hard-blocks that too, independent of which op it rides on.\n\n### Three ESCALATE (SOFT) gates plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-compliance-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (permanent member of `always-escalate-ops`; `:decide` can\n  never route it directly to `:commit`). This is the ONLY channel through\n  which any assessment/penalty/audit-worthy observation reaches a human --\n  the actor itself never decides the underlying compliance question.\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` whose `:order-amount` exceeds\n  `supply-order-cost-threshold` (2000) -- ESCALATES (human sign-off), not\n  a HARD block, since routine office procurement is categorically\n  different from the enforcement-authority risk this ADR guards against.\n- low confidence (< `confidence-floor` 0.6) escalates, for any op.\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, explicitly named in this ADR's task brief)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"assessment\", \"penalty\", \"audit\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default/legitimate rationale text, self-blocking the happy\npath -- especially risky here because `:flag-compliance-concern`'s whole\npurpose is to discuss compliance risk using words like \"penalty\" or\n\"assessment\" in the ordinary, legitimate, DESCRIPTIVE course of flagging\nthem for human review, and `:schedule-audit-appointment` routinely and\ncorrectly uses the word \"audit\". `taxexcise.governor/enforcement-scope-phrases`\nis phrased exclusively as finalization/execution ACTION phrases (\"finalize\nthe tax assessment\", \"impose the penalty\", \"order the collection action\",\n\"order the lien action\"), never bare nouns.\n`taxexcise.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip` is\na dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed allowlist,\ndriven through the default mock advisor (`taxexcise.advisor/infer`) --\nincluding one case whose caller-supplied `:concern-detail` explicitly\ncontains the bare nouns \"penalty\" AND \"assessment\" in ordinary descriptive\nusage (\"repeated late filings suggest a penalty and assessment review may\nbe warranted\") -- never trips `enforcement-scope-violation?`. A separate\npair of governor-level tests independently confirm the TRUE-POSITIVE side:\nhand-crafted proposals whose `:rationale`/`:concern-detail` actually say\n\"finalize the tax assessment now\" or \"impose the penalty immediately\" are\nstill HARD blocked, proving the check is not vacuous. An actor-level\nintegration test additionally drives a rogue advisor stub that proposes\n`:op :finalize-tax-assessment` end-to-end through the compiled graph and\nasserts the run lands on `:hold`, never `:commit`, with the ledger's hold\nentry citing `:op-not-allowlisted` -- proving the guarantee holds through\nthe full graph, not only at the governor unit-test level.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"3352\" ...}` entry is\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set\n  to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3352` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3352`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented count\n  bumped accordingly (recomputed from the live registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary` immediately before the registry\n  edit, not by `grep -c`, to account for concurrent sibling landings in\n  this same batch of 6 parallel ISCO agents). `maturity-summary`\n  immediately before this ADR was drafted (may drift further before the\n  registry edit itself lands, due to sibling concurrency):\n  `{:total 436, :spec 215, :blueprint 0, :implemented 221}`.\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`) on a fresh scaffold in a\n  uniquely-named scratch directory (never the shared checkout): 24 tests,\n  54 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified after\n  push and again after a fresh re-clone.\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- `taxexcise.store` uses two independently-registered entities (`filer`\n  and `office`) rather than the reference's `client`+`account` pair,\n  because this domain genuinely has two unrelated provenance roots: some\n  ops concern a specific taxpayer/filer (`:log-filing-record`,\n  `:flag-compliance-concern`) and others concern the tax office's own\n  logistics (`:schedule-audit-appointment`, `:coordinate-supply-order`)\n  with no taxpayer involved at all -- a deliberate domain adaptation, not\n  a structural copy of the reference's single-client-multi-account shape.\n- This session confirmed `90-docs/adr` is EDN-only (commit `6d2d0eeda658`)\n  and confirmed no `kotoba-lang/technology` deps.edn dependency is used by\n  actor repos, matching this batch's other sibling ADRs' independent\n  findings of the same two facts.\n- The reference repo's own README links to a\n  `docs/samples/operator-console.html` file that does not exist anywhere\n  in that repository's history -- a broken/aspirational link in the\n  verified reference. This actor's README does not repeat that claim (no\n  `docs/samples/` directory was scaffolded).\n- `CONTRIBUTING.md` in this actor documents only `clojure -M:test` (the\n  alias that actually exists in `deps.edn`), correcting the reference's\n  inherited `clojure -M:dev:test`/`clojure -M:lint` references to aliases\n  that do not exist in the reference's own `deps.edn` either -- a small,\n  deliberate deviation from strict mirroring since propagating a\n  known-wrong command into a fresh repo's contributor docs serves no\n  purpose.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Gating enforcement-shaped ops behind the escalation path instead of\n  omitting them from the allowlist entirely: rejected outright per the\n  task brief -- \"These must be structurally absent from the op-allowlist\n  entirely, not merely gated behind escalation\" is an explicit, doubled-\n  down requirement, not a style preference. A gated-but-present op would\n  still let a sufficiently-confident advisor construct a technically-valid\n  proposal for it; a structurally-absent op cannot be constructed as a\n  recognized proposal at all.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"assessment\" \"penalty\"\n  \"audit\" \"collection\" \"lien\"]`): rejected per the fleet-known self-trip\n  bug class and this ADR's own task brief, which named the exact failure\n  mode and required a dedicated regression test. Phrased as finalization/\n  execution ACTIONS instead.\n- Making `:appointment-capacity-exceeded` an ESCALATE gate instead of\n  HARD: rejected -- staffing/logistics capacity is a hard physical\n  constraint (an office cannot host more appointments than it has\n  capacity for), unlike the supply-order cost threshold, which is a\n  business-judgment threshold appropriately left to human sign-off.\n- Single `filer`/`account`-shaped store mirroring the reference exactly,\n  forcing office-scoped ops to reference a synthetic \"account\" owned by a\n  placeholder filer: rejected as needlessly indirect and domain-dishonest\n  -- office logistics (appointment scheduling, supply orders) genuinely\n  has no taxpayer/filer party, so a `filer`+`office` two-entity model is\n  the more honest structural adaptation.\n\n## References\n\n- `cloud-itonami-isco-3313/` (structural reference, explicitly named in\n  this ADR's task brief -- cloned and read in full in this session)\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` `resources/kotoba/occupation/registry.edn`\n  `\"3352\"` entry\n- ADR-2607121000 (Wave definition, reverse-toposort rollout plan -- Wave 2)\n- ADR-2790003343 (`cloud-itonami-isco-3343`, sibling in this same\n  occupation-classification batch -- schema and live-convention precedent\n  this ADR follows most closely, including the same self-trip-guard bug\n  class named independently in both ADRs' task briefs)\n")))
